click API key create new API key

62
00:03:04.054 --> 00:03:06.657
Let us build a practical simulation toolkit

63
00:03:06.657 --> 00:03:07.417
on Ubuntu.

64
00:03:07.838 --> 00:03:10.021
We will install the system utilities,

65
00:03:10.281 --> 00:03:13.264
CalculiX and Gmsh, OpenCode two,

66
00:03:13.605 --> 00:03:15.467
and a separate Python environment.

67
00:03:15.768 --> 00:03:17.189
Follow the four stages,

68
00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:19.572
then check that the tools are available.

69
00:03:20.653 --> 00:03:23.536
This walkthrough uses Ubuntu twenty four point

70
00:03:23.536 --> 00:03:25.878
zero four, with a Bash terminal,

71
00:03:25.919 --> 00:03:28.781
an internet connection, and sudo access.

72
00:03:29.502 --> 00:03:31.144
Open Terminal with Control,

73
00:03:31.324 --> 00:03:31.924
Alt, T.

74
00:03:32.225 --> 00:03:34.247
Create a working folder and enter it.

75
00:03:34.687 --> 00:03:36.949
The terminal shown here is an animation;

76
00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:39.692
download times and output will vary.

77
00:03:40.833 --> 00:03:44.035
First, refresh the package list with sudo

78
00:03:44.035 --> 00:03:44.816
apt update.

79
00:03:45.376 --> 00:03:47.297
If Ubuntu asks for your password,

80
00:03:47.298 --> 00:03:49.119
type it and press Enter.

81
00:03:49.479 --> 00:03:52.041
The terminal does not show password characters.

82
00:03:52.221 --> 00:03:54.222
Wait for the command to finish before

83
00:03:54.222 --> 00:03:54.923
continuing.

84
00:03:55.283 --> 00:03:57.704
This updates the package index; it does

85
00:03:57.704 --> 00:03:59.866
not upgrade every installed program.

86
00:04:01.006 --> 00:04:02.888
Now install Git, curl,

87
00:04:03.048 --> 00:04:05.711
jq, and the Python environment tools.

88
00:04:06.031 --> 00:04:08.914
We also include the repository helper and

89
00:04:08.954 --> 00:04:12.637
HTTPS certificates, which are useful on minimal

90
00:04:12.637 --> 00:04:14.018
Ubuntu installations.

91
00:04:14.379 --> 00:04:16.901
The backslashes continue the same command onto

92
00:04:16.901 --> 00:04:17.742
the next line.

93
00:04:17.962 --> 00:04:20.384
The dash y option automatically accepts the

94
00:04:20.384 --> 00:04:21.505
package confirmation.

95
00:04:22.727 --> 00:04:25.509
Next, enable the Universe repository,

96
00:04:25.649 --> 00:04:28.092
where Ubuntu provides these science packages.

97
00:04:28.652 --> 00:04:32.276
Run sudo add apt repository universe,

98
00:04:32.376 --> 00:04:34.899
confirm if prompted, and refresh the package

99
00:04:34.899 --> 00:04:35.679
list again.

100
00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:37.762
If Universe is already enabled,

101
00:04:37.762 --> 00:04:38.722
you can continue.

102
00:04:38.903 --> 00:04:41.405
Package versions depend on your Ubuntu release.

103
00:04:42.606 --> 00:04:45.469
Install CalculiX C C X,

104
00:04:45.629 --> 00:04:47.551
CalculiX C G X,

105
00:04:47.811 --> 00:04:48.792
and Gmsh.

106
00:04:49.052 --> 00:04:51.394
Gmsh creates geometry and meshes.

107
00:04:51.555 --> 00:04:53.877
C C X solves the finite element

108
00:04:53.877 --> 00:04:56.159
model, while C G X supports pre

109
00:04:56.159 --> 00:04:57.320
and post processing.

110
00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:00.083
Installing these programs does not yet connect

111
00:05:00.083 --> 00:05:02.265
them to an AI agent or validate

112
00:05:02.265 --> 00:05:03.125
a simulation.

113
00:05:04.126 --> 00:05:07.430
For OpenCode two, use the official installer

114
00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:08.151
shown here.

115
00:05:08.371 --> 00:05:09.973
Curl downloads the script,

116
00:05:10.173 --> 00:05:11.935
and the pipe sends it to Bash

117
00:05:11.935 --> 00:05:12.496
to run.

118
00:05:12.776 --> 00:05:14.759
Review the installer from the official website

119
00:05:14.759 --> 00:05:15.880
before executing it.

120
00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:17.762
Run this as your normal user.

121
00:05:18.002 --> 00:05:19.584
This is the version two address from

122
00:05:19.584 --> 00:05:20.465
your command sheet.

123
00:05:21.606 --> 00:05:23.407
Reload your Bash configuration,

124
00:05:23.668 --> 00:05:25.188
or open a new terminal,

125
00:05:25.629 --> 00:05:27.690
then check the OpenCode version.

126
00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:29.451
If the command is not found,

127
00:05:29.531 --> 00:05:31.633
follow the path instructions printed by the

128
00:05:31.633 --> 00:05:32.333
installer.

129
00:05:33.274 --> 00:05:35.575
Installing the application does not configure a

130
00:05:35.575 --> 00:05:36.536
model provider.

131
00:05:36.576 --> 00:05:39.998
Provider sign in and any associated usage

132
00:05:39.998 --> 00:05:41.159
charges are separate.

133
00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:43.781
Back in your working folder,

134
00:05:43.781 --> 00:05:45.982
create a virtual environment named F E

135
00:05:46.022 --> 00:05:47.482
M, then activate it.

136
00:05:47.742 --> 00:05:50.243
This keeps project packages separate from Ubuntu

137
00:05:50.243 --> 00:05:51.224
system Python.

138
00:05:51.424 --> 00:05:53.545
The prompt usually gains the environment name

139
00:05:53.545 --> 00:05:54.365
in brackets.

140
00:05:54.645 --> 00:05:56.666
Activate this environment whenever you work on

141
00:05:56.666 --> 00:05:58.306
the project in a new terminal.

142
00:05:59.346 --> 00:06:00.727
With the environment active,

143
00:06:01.307 --> 00:06:03.328
install the five Python libraries.

144
00:06:03.969 --> 00:06:06.950
Using python dash m pip ensures that

145
00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:09.171
pip belongs to the active interpreter.

146
00:06:10.132 --> 00:06:12.953
NumPy and pandas handle numerical data,

147
00:06:13.293 --> 00:06:15.374
and Matplotlib makes plots.

148
00:06:15.795 --> 00:06:18.456
The OpenAI and M C P libraries

149
00:06:18.536 --> 00:06:21.758
support integrations; installing them alone does
not

150
00:06:21.758 --> 00:06:23.739
configure an API connection.

151
00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:27.022
Now check the executable paths,

152
00:06:27.503 --> 00:06:29.765
import all five Python packages,

153
00:06:30.045 --> 00:06:31.266
and run pip check.

154
00:06:31.747 --> 00:06:33.949
Each command should finish without an error.

155
00:06:34.189 --> 00:06:36.031
These are basic installation checks,

156
00:06:36.291 --> 00:06:38.093
not proof that a solver model is

157
00:06:38.093 --> 00:06:38.593
correct.

158
00:06:38.974 --> 00:06:41.736
Gmsh and C G X graphical windows

159
00:06:41.736 --> 00:06:44.039
also need a working desktop or display

160
00:06:44.039 --> 00:06:44.719
connection.

161
00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:46.801
For your next session,

162
00:06:47.021 --> 00:06:49.183
return to the working folder and activate

163
00:06:49.223 --> 00:06:50.104
F E M again.

164
00:06:50.224 --> 00:06:51.945
When finished, use deactivate.

165
00:06:52.186 --> 00:06:55.048
If pip reports an externally managed environment,

166
00:06:55.148 --> 00:06:57.250
check that you activated the virtual environment

167
00:06:57.250 --> 00:06:57.730
first.

168
00:06:57.830 --> 00:06:59.752
Keep project installs inside it,

169
00:06:59.872 --> 00:07:01.093
without sudo pip.

170
00:07:02.213 --> 00:07:04.275
You now have the installation steps for

171
00:07:04.275 --> 00:07:05.937
the toolkit in your command sheet.

172
00:07:06.297 --> 00:07:08.600
Keep the commands and checks for repeatable

173
00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:09.120
setup.

174
00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:12.503
Next, build one small mesh and solver

175
00:07:12.503 --> 00:07:14.285
example, verify the result,

176
00:07:14.345 --> 00:07:16.487
and only then expand the automation.

177
00:07:16.968 --> 00:07:19.110
The command sheet and captions are included

178
00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:20.331
with this tutorial.

179
00:07:21.954 --> 00:07:23.496
Let us turn the theory

180
00:07:23.616 --> 00:07:26.501
into a practical finite element workflow.

181
00:07:26.902 --> 00:07:30.868
We will use Gmsh, CalculiX, and Python.

182
00:07:31.389 --> 00:07:33.172
Each tool has a clear job.

183
00:07:34.213 --> 00:07:35.935
Gmsh builds the geometry

184
00:07:35.935 --> 00:07:37.116
and generates the mesh.

185
00:07:37.497 --> 00:07:40.699
CalculiX then solves the finite element model,

186
00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:42.841
for structural or thermal problems.

187
00:07:43.402 --> 00:07:44.864
Keeping those jobs separate

188
00:07:44.864 --> 00:07:47.226
makes the workflow easier to inspect and
repeat.

189
00:07:48.107 --> 00:07:49.689
Here is the Ubuntu setup.

190
00:07:49.870 --> 00:07:51.653
Install the mesher and solver,

191
00:07:51.954 --> 00:07:54.799
add OpenCode, and create a Python environment.

192
00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:56.943
These commands give us the tools

193
00:07:56.943 --> 00:07:57.905
for the workflow.

194
00:07:58.626 --> 00:08:00.668
Think of the calculation in three steps.

195
00:08:01.048 --> 00:08:03.410
First, generate the mesh with Gmsh.

196
00:08:03.851 --> 00:08:06.573
Next, solve the physics with CalculiX.

197
00:08:07.113 --> 00:08:09.215
Then, use Python to validate

198
00:08:09.215 --> 00:08:10.576
and visualize the results.

199
00:08:10.636 --> 00:08:11.697
Remember the sequence:

200
00:08:11.998 --> 00:08:13.559
mesh, solve, and validate.

201
00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:17.584
An AI agent can prepare simulation files,

202
00:08:17.844 --> 00:08:21.268
run commands, and help analyze solver output.

203
00:08:21.669 --> 00:08:23.030
But we still have to check

204
00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:26.054
the boundary conditions, mesh convergence,

205
00:08:26.314 --> 00:08:27.575
and physical accuracy.

206
00:08:27.996 --> 00:08:30.559
Automation does not replace verification.

207
00:08:31.801 --> 00:08:33.463
Start with one reliable,

208
00:08:33.483 --> 00:08:35.646
reproducible simulation workflow.

209
00:08:35.947 --> 00:08:36.908
Check it carefully.

210
00:08:37.329 --> 00:08:40.233
Then introduce more advanced AI automation.

211
00:08:40.874 --> 00:08:42.597
Build trust in the calculation

212
00:08:42.597 --> 00:08:44.179
before expanding the automation.
